---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import LoadBalancerReference from '../../components/LoadBalancerReference.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Load Balancer Algorithms Reference" description="A quick reference comparing common load balancing algorithms — round robin, least connections, IP hash, and more.">
  <LoadBalancerReference client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why algorithm choice affects more than raw throughput</h2>
  <p>Different algorithms make different assumptions about your backend servers and traffic patterns — round robin assumes uniform capacity and request cost, while least connections adapts better to long-lived or variable-duration requests. Picking the wrong one can create uneven load even when the algorithm is "working correctly."</p>

  <h2>How to use this reference</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Consider your backend servers' relative capacity and your requests' typical duration variability.</li>
    <li>Match the algorithm to your actual traffic characteristics.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common Load Balancing Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Using round robin with heterogeneous server capacity</strong> without weighting — a weaker server gets the same share of traffic as a much more powerful one.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming IP hash guarantees perfectly even distribution</strong> — it provides session affinity, but distribution evenness depends on client IP diversity.</li>
    <li><strong>Not reconsidering algorithm choice as traffic patterns change</strong> over time — what worked at launch may not fit traffic patterns a year later.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Reference Load Balancer Algorithms</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Choosing a load balancing algorithm when configuring a new load balancer</li>
    <li>Diagnosing uneven load distribution across backend servers</li>
    <li>Deciding whether session affinity (IP hash) is needed for your application</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Load Balancer Algorithm FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>What's a good default if I'm not sure?</strong> Round robin (or weighted round robin for uneven server capacity) is a reasonable, simple default for many applications without special session-affinity requirements.</p>
</ToolLayout>
